# tools/absolute-paths.allow — the absolute local paths this repository already carried, and why.
#
# tools/absolute-paths.mjs refuses a commit that names a folder on the machine that wrote it. Every file below
# was written before that gate existed, and its content is a record: rewriting a landing note to scrub a path
# would change what it says happened. So they are allowed, one line each, and the gate still refuses anything
# NEW. Nothing here is a place a reader can open, and nothing here is a template to copy.
#
#   <path> | <count> | <reason>
#
#   <path>   repo-relative, forward slashes, EXACT — no globs, because a glob allows the next file too.
#   <count>  the paths that file held when it was recorded. The gate fails when a file holds MORE than this,
#            so a frozen record cannot quietly grow a second, newer path. Removing one is always allowed:
#            lower the count to match and the record stays true.
#   <reason> one line saying why the path is there. An empty reason is a refusal, not a pass.
#
# A new landing does not belong here. Take the path out of the line, or describe the file instead of naming
# where it sat. `node tools/absolute-paths.mjs --draft` prints the lines for anything it found.

